Where the Dockerfile Comes Into Existence
The Dockerfile is not shown in your Jenkinsfile, but it's a critical component that must exist in your project. Here's how it fits in:

Location: Typically in your project root (ImmunizationTracker/Dockerfile)

When it's used: During the "Docker Build & Push" stage when docker.build() is called

What it does: Contains instructions for building your application image:

Base image (e.g., openjdk)

Copying your JAR file

Setting entry points

Configuring ports

Example Dockerfile might look like:

dockerfile
FROM openjdk:21-jdk
COPY target/your-app.jar app.jar
EXPOSE 8080
ENTRYPOINT ["java","-jar","/app.jar"]
The Jenkins pipeline automatically looks for this Dockerfile when building the image. The Dockerfile is essential because it defines how your application should be containerized.
